Include <windows.h> before <stringapiset.h> in nasmlib/file.c.

New in 3.02, the _WIN32 branch of `nasmlib/file.c` calls
`MultiByteToWideChar()` and reaches for `<stringapiset.h>` to declare it.
That is one of the Windows SDK's "minwin" leaf headers, and it is not
self-contained: its include chain reaches `<winnt.h>` without anything
having established the SDK's target-architecture macro (`_AMD64_`,
`_X86_`, `_ARM64_`, ...), which the compiler does not predefine -- cl.exe
only predefines `_M_AMD64`/`_M_X64`. `<winnt.h>` then hits its closing

    #else
    #error "No Target Architecture"
    #endif

and the translation unit dies with C1189.

`<windows.h>` is the header that sets this up, which is why the leaf
headers work for everyone who includes it first and only for them. nasm
includes it nowhere, so pull it in here. WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN keeps it to
the base API and, in particular, keeps `wingdi.h` from defining `ERROR`
on top of nasm's own error vocabulary.

--- a/nasmlib/file.c
+++ b/nasmlib/file.c
@@ -45,6 +45,10 @@
  */
 #ifdef _WIN32
 #include <wchar.h>
+#ifndef WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN
+# define WIN32_LEAN_AND_MEAN 1
+#endif
+#include <windows.h>
 #include <stringapiset.h>

 typedef wchar_t *os_filename;
